<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Empire of Nicaea</h1><p class="st-meta">1204 CE – 1261 CE · capital Nicaea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest Byzantine successor state after 1204, which rebuilt Byzantine power in Anatolia and recaptured Constantinople in 1261.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-laskaris">Theodore Laskaris</a>, son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-angelos">Alexios III</a>, gathered refugees and troops in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bithynia">Bithynia</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> as his base he fought off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latins">Latins</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, and in 1208 had himself crowned emperor by a newly elected patriarch, giving his state the strongest claim to Byzantine legitimacy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laskarid-dynasty">Laskarid</a> emperors governed through a Byzantine-style court and bureaucracy, with the patriarch residing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> and the court often at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nymphaion">Nymphaion</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Vatatzes</a> promoted agricultural self-sufficiency, restricted luxury imports, founded charities and expanded into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 1246. Scholarship flourished under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-blemmydes">Nikephoros Blemmydes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-ii-laskaris">Theodore II Laskaris</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> seized power from the boy emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-laskaris">John IV</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> and Epirotes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Pelagonia</a> in 1259 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in July 1261, restoring the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Neglect of the Anatolian frontier after the move opened the way to Turkish expansion.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/empire-of-nicaea.jpg" alt="Empire of Nicaea" width="320" height="169"><figcaption>Image: LatinEmpire · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LatinEmpire2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1204 CE – 1261 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nicaea</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Theodore I Laskaris, John III Vatatzes, Michael VIII Palaiologos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nicaean Empire</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1232&amp;focus=empire-of-nicaea" aria-label="Empire of Nicaea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5410 733 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Empire of Nicaea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5730 989L5483 954M5730 989L5710 1120M5730 989L5709 970M5730 989L5709 970M5730 989L5563 982"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5483 954h0M5710 1120h0M5709 970h0M5709 970h0M5563 982h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5730 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5730 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nicaea. <a href="/atlas?year=1232&amp;focus=empire-of-nicaea">On